<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      Read the ES-DE folders.

      ES-DE on Android keeps its library in ordinary places — /storage/emulated/0/ES-DE
      and /storage/emulated/0/ROMs — and this app is pointed at them rather than
      keeping a second copy. Since Android 11 an app cannot open arbitrary files
      there with the ordinary storage permissions; it needs this one, which the
      user grants by a toggle in system settings rather than a dialog. ES-DE's
      own Android app asks for exactly the same thing.

      maxSdkVersion on the legacy pair: they do nothing from Android 13 on, and
      leaving them unbounded reads as if they still did.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="29" />

    <!-- AndroidTV support -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.software.leanback" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      The emulators this app talks to.

      Since Android 11 an app cannot see that another package exists unless it
      says so here: `getPackageInfo` throws NameNotFoundException and the answer
      is indistinguishable from "not installed". That is exactly what happened —
      the settings page reported RetroArch missing on a device it was installed
      on.

      Both RetroArch names, because a device may carry either build. This is
      also what a launch will need: an Intent to a package you cannot see does
      not resolve.
    -->
    <queries>
        <!--
          Every emulator ES-DE knows how to start, so this app can see which of
          them are here.

          Since Android 11 a package it cannot see is a package that does not
          exist: `getPackageInfo` throws and `resolveActivity` answers null, so
          a standalone emulator that is installed reads as missing and the
          launch silently falls back to RetroArch. That is why this app could
          only ever start RetroArch.

          The list is `android_packages` in data/esde-core-map.json, which is
          ES-DE's own `es_find_rules.xml`. Regenerate both together.
        -->
